## 3. End-to-end data flow (INF → service → worker → UWP AppService → OSD)

1. **Firmware**: the physical camera shutter sets EC byte `LSKD` (`20-acpi/dsl/DSDT.dsl:14823,14833`)
   and the camera-port gate `CAMS` (`DSDT.dsl:13785`) which drives `HSP3._UPC` (`:6744`) and
   `CAMA._STA` (`:6830`).
2. **ACPI notify**: firmware issues `Notify (WMIU, 0xD0)` (`DSDT.dsl:14851`); `WMIU._WED`
   (`:24676`) converts `LSKD` to a WMI event value.
3. **WMI event**: delivered as `ROOT\WMI` event class `LENOVO_UTILITY_EVENT` to
   `FnHotkeyUtility.exe` (`CMoniterWmiUtilityEvent`, strings16-fnhotkeyutility.exe.txt:670-674;
   `strings-fnhotkeyutility.exe.txt` shows `SELECT * FROM LENOVO_UTILITY_EVENT`).
4. **Worker decode**: `MsgLogic::g_HandleEvent` (`…:900`) reads the event payload and derives the
   camera state from a bit: `MsgLogic g_HandleEvent SwitchCameraStatus in ....` (`:902`),
   `m_bExistRFDevice&8 pInfoData->Data[0] %d` (`:904-905`), `camera unkown status!` (`:906`),
   producing `e_Event_CAMERA_SWITCH_ON`/`e_Event_CAMERA_SWITCH_OFF` (`:1000-1001`).
   The Windows camera-privacy setting is then **written** through a COM camera-control object:
   `CameraPrivacy SwitchCameraStatus CoCreateInstance … pMoniker[nIndexCamera]->BindToObject …
   pCameraControl[i]->Set` (`:410-414`).
5. **Background blur (separate feature)**: event `e_Event_CAMERA_BG_BLUR` (`:1027`) →
   `BlurSwitchCLR.Switch()` (managed method inside fnhotkey.exe;
   `strings-fnhotkeyutility.exe.txt:3074` `BlurSwitchCLR.BlurSwitchCLR.Switch`, log
   `e_Event_CAMERA_BG_BLUR BlurSwitchCLR Switch retBlur=%d` `:1060`) →
   `BlurSwitchCS.dll` `ToggleBlurSwitch()` → UWP AppService.
   If it failed/absent, fnhotkey falls back to launching `lenovo-smartappearance:backgroundsettingpage`
   or the Store URL `ms-windows-store://pdp/?productid=9NRLFDZ54PZB` (`:1081-1085`).
6. **UWP side**: package `E0469640.SmartAppearance_5grkq8ppsgwt4`, AppService
   `Lenovo.CameraControlBackgroundService` (`strings16-blurswitchcs.dll.txt:2-3`) applies the blur
   and returns `Result = BlurOn | BlurOff | BlurError`.
7. **OSD**: drawn by FnHotkeyUtility's own window; camera OSD gate is `CAMERA_BIT7` in the hotkey
   config plus `SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\LenovoFnAndFunctionKeys\ControlShowOSD`
   (`:892,901,908`).

## 4. Kernel-mode driver: REFUTED

- `glob **/*.sys` over the whole workspace → **no files**.
- INF has no `ServiceBinary … .sys` entry; its only service is the user exe (`inf:73-78`).
- The `.cat` member list (§1) contains the 16 files above and no `.sys`.
- fnhotkey references `EMDriver CKernelLib` / `DeviceIoControl IOCTL_ENERGYDRV_*`
  (`strings16-fnhotkeyutility.exe.txt:438-462`) i.e. it *talks to* a kernel driver named
  `EMDriver`, but that driver is **not shipped in this package** (it is a separate Lenovo
  energy-management driver installed by other packages). [INFERENCE] It is absent here.

## 5. Questions 1–4 (brief)

**Q1 — AppService name / package family name.**
AppService = `Lenovo.CameraControlBackgroundService`; package family = `E0469640.SmartAppearance_5grkq8ppsgwt4`
(`strings16-blurswitchcs.dll.txt:2-3`; also `strings16-fnhotkeyutility.exe.txt:1081`).
⚠ Correction to STATE.md: the DLL does **not** connect to
`E0469640.LenovoUtility_5grkq8ppsgwt4!LenovoUtility`. That AUMID appears only in
`FnHotkeyUtility.exe` (`strings16-fnhotkeyutility.exe.txt:854`) and only as
`ActivateApplication()` for the superkey (launch the LenovoUtility app), not as an AppService.

**Q2 — exact request keys/values and status codes.**
Request `ValueSet`: `"Key"` = `"ToggleBlurSwitch"`. Response `ValueSet`: `"Result"` ∈
`{ "BlurOn", "BlurOff", "BlurError" }`. Status constants (return `int`):
`STATUS_SET_BLUR_OFF_OK, STATUS_SET_BLUR_ON_OK, STATUS_EXCEPTION, STATUS_SVR_REPOND_ERR [sic],
STATUS_SET_FEATURE_ERR, STATUS_RETURN_ERR, STATUS_CONNECT_SVR_ERR, STATUS_SET_TIMEOUT`
(`strings-blurswitchcs.dll.txt:9-16`; `strings16-blurswitchcs.dll.txt:4-6`).
Numeric values are not present in metadata (see §B caveat).

**Q3 — support DLLs / package identities.**
`ludp.dll`, `ludp.net.dll`, `controllib.dll`, `intelcontrollib.dll`, `mmstate.dll` contain **no**
camera/privacy/blur/LVF/SmartAppearance/AppService strings at all (grep over
`70-evidence/strings*(-…).txt`). `atiadlxx.dll` matches only GPU-API symbol names
`ADL2_ApplicationProfiles_DriverAreaPrivacy_Get`, `ADL2_CVML_Camera_DMFT_Set`
(unrelated to the camera privacy switch).
- `E0469640.LenovoUtility_5grkq8ppsgwt4!LenovoUtility` → only fnhotkey:854; activates the
  LenovoUtility UWP app for the superkey.
- `E046963F.LenovoCompanion_k1h2ywk1493x8` → fnhotkey:832; Lenovo Companion/Vantage/PcManager launch.
  Also `E046963F.AIMeetingManager_k1h2ywk1493x8` (:625).
- The camera/blur feature is **owned by the `E0469640.SmartAppearance_5grkq8ppsgwt4` UWP app**
  (AppService `Lenovo.CameraControlBackgroundService`), invoked from `BlurSwitchCS.dll` which is
  loaded by `FnHotkeyUtility.exe`. FnHotkeyUtility also has its own camera-privacy path and the
  camera-switch event handling.

**Q4 — is the camera switch STATE ever read, or only blur toggled?**
**No component in this package reads the hardware switch position as a value.** Evidence:
- `BlurSwitchCS.dll` exposes only `OpenAsync`, `SendMessageAsync`, `ToggleBlurSwitch` — there is no
  getter/query method; it sends a toggle command and returns a *status code*.
- The switch status in fnhotkey is derived **from the WMI event payload**, not from a device query:
  `MsgLogic g_HandleEvent SwitchCameraStatus in ....` (:902),
  `m_bExistRFDevice&8 pInfoData->Data[0] %d` (:904-905), `camera unkown status!` (:906).
- The `CameraPrivacy …` path calls `pCameraControl[i]->Set` (:413) — a **write** of the Windows
  camera-privacy setting, not a read of hardware state.
- [INFERENCE] The only component that could read the authoritative hardware state is the
  SmartAppearance UWP app (owner of the AppService) — it is **not** in this package; the Windows
  side never exposes a queryable "switch" value other than the transient event bit.
